      {
         "id": "SECTION_introduction",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Introduction\n\nKnowing when to plant and harvest—that is, practicing agriculture—has been part of human life for at least 10,000 years, and it directly shapes who we are today (<htcite0>; <htcite1>).\n\nOne of the first steps in this process was to determine the length of a year. In this text we will treat a year as 365 days, even though it really lasts 365 days and 6 hours (<htcite2>).\n\nWorking with such large numbers introduces inaccuracies, which is why we split the year into smaller intervals, such as seasons and weeks.\n\nBefore moving on, let us reflect once more on these divisions:",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "b6f1314e-d867-4e20-9934-1b37360b562e",
                     "text": "Marlowe, F. W.",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2005",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "a509cc9b-fbf4-43f1-be9d-9fc3faf0d1da",
                     "text": "Marlowe, F. W.",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "1995",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "74430d2a-89e1-487d-bc92-fa19b0586ded",
                     "text": "The Editors of Encyclopaedia Britannica",
                     "page": "Year",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "1",
                        "day": "26"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection 1: On Precision</b><br /><br />When we use a 365-day year, we introduce an error of 6 hours, which is corrected every four years with a 366-day year.<br /><br />If we counted a year as a total number of weeks (7 days each), would we be closer to or farther from the actual length of a year?</div></div></center>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_amorite",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Amorites\n\n#### Origin\n\nThe Amorites were a Semitic people, apparently from western Mesopotamia (in what is now Syria), although an origin in the Arabian Peninsula cannot be ruled out (Figure 1). They first appear in the historical record before the third millennium BCE, according to the Gregorian calendar (<htcite0>; <htcite1>).",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "170e2f48-57f2-4a74-ae07-547e10a367b0",
                     "text": "Mark, J. J.",
                     "page": "Amorite",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2011",
                        "month": "4",
                        "day": "28"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "4d65b57d-f36a-4c58-a4ff-cf1a3857c4ed",
                     "text": "The Editors of Encyclopaedia Britannica",
                     "page": "Amorite",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2014",
                        "month": "4",
                        "day": "17"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": [
                  {
                     "type": "gregory",
                     "year": "-1600",
                     "month": "",
                     "day": ""
                  },
                  {
                     "type": "gregory",
                     "year": "-1100",
                     "month": "",
                     "day": ""
                  }
               ],
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"desc\"><img src=\"\" id=\"imgME\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('imgME', '0%')\" class=\"imgcenter\"/><b>Figure 1</b>: Map of the Middle East region. © Mapswire (<htcite0>).</p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "10cd855b-c2dc-4045-8a6c-f8486ef67cfa",
                     "text": "Mapswire",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "#### Rise and Decline\n\nThe Amorites dominated the history of Mesopotamia, Syria, and Palestine during the second millennium BCE. The Babylonian kings of this period, such as Hammurabi, belonged to this people (<htcite0>; <htcite1>).\n\nBetween <htdate0> and <htdate1> we find no records of the Amorite language being used in the region, although this does not mean that their civilization disappeared.\n\nGroups called \"Amorites\" reappeared and clashed with the Neo-Assyrian Empire. There is also evidence that they influenced other peoples, such as the Hebrews (<htcite2>; <htcite3>).\n\n#### Hammurabi\n\nA dedicated text on Hammurabi will be published in the *Genealogical Trees* section, but his famous code of laws is worth mentioning here: written around <htdate2>, a copy of it is preserved in the British Museum (Figure 2). This code shows notable similarities to Jewish texts written more than a millennium later (<htcite4>; <htcite5>).",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "170e2f48-57f2-4a74-ae07-547e10a367b0",
                     "text": "Mark, J. J.",
                     "page": "Amorite",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2011",
                        "month": "4",
                        "day": "28"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "4d65b57d-f36a-4c58-a4ff-cf1a3857c4ed",
                     "text": "The Editors of Encyclopaedia Britannica",
                     "page": "Amorite",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2014",
                        "month": "4",
                        "day": "17"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "d3baf59f-4cff-4f57-ab9f-5d35143b993a",
                     "text": "LEWY, H., & LEWY, J.",
                     "page": "pp. 1 - 145",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
