      {
         "id": "SECTION_pentencondat",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Pentecostal Calendar\n\nAccording to studies by Lewy Hildegard and Lewy Julius, a calendar based on seven periods of 50 days—known as the *Pentecostal Calendar*—was used in Assyria, Babylon, and Palestine. This calendar also included an extra 15-day period, called *šapattum*, to complete the year (<htcite0>).\n\n#### Calendar Verification\n\nLet us check whether this year structure is a good approximation of the real solar year. To do so, we revisit the multiplication tables for 7 and 10 that we studied earlier:",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "d3baf59f-4cff-4f57-ab9f-5d35143b993a",
                     "text": "LEWY, H., & LEWY, J.",
                     "page": "pp. 50 - 51, 84",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "1942",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><div id=\"mParent7\" style=\"margin: auto; text-align: center;\"></div></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><div id=\"mParent10\" style=\"margin: auto; text-align: center;\"></div></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Next, we multiply 50 by 7 to verify the length of the longest period in the Pentecostal Calendar:",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p style=\"text-align=center\"><center>&nbsp;&nbsp; <span class=\"multexample\" id=\"mmtc2\">5</span><span class=\"multexample\" id=\"mmtc1\">0</span> <br />× &nbsp;&nbsp;<span class=\"multexample\" id=\"mmbc1\">7 </span><br />_____<br /></span>&nbsp; <span class=\"multexample\" id=\"mmrc2\">35</span><span class=\"multexample\" id=\"mmrc1\">0 </span></center></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Calculation 1</b>: Multiplication of 50 by 7.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "We then add the 15 days of the *šapattum* to the result:",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p style=\"text-align=center\"><center>&nbsp;&nbsp; <span class=\"sumexample1\" id=\"cmtc3\">3</span><span class=\"sumexample1\" id=\"cmtc2\">5</span><span class=\"sumexample1\" id=\"cmtc1\">0 </span> <br />+ &nbsp;&nbsp;<span class=\"sumexample1\" id=\"cmbc2\">1</span><span class=\"sumexample1\" id=\"cmbc1\">5 </span><br />_____<br /></span>&nbsp; <span class=\"sumexample1\" id=\"cmrc3\">3</span><span class=\"sumexample1\" id=\"cmrc2\">6</span><span class=\"sumexample1\" id=\"cmrc1\">5 </span></center></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Calculation 2</b>: Sum of 350 and 15.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Thus, we conclude that the Pentecostal Calendar comes very close to the length of a solar year (365 days); the remaining error is about 6 hours (<htcite0>).\n\nTo make this easier to picture, here is a chart with the number of days in each period:",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "74430d2a-89e1-487d-bc92-fa19b0586ded",
                     "text": "The Editors of Encyclopaedia Britannica",
                     "page": "Year",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "1",
                        "day": "26"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><div class=\"htchart\"><canvas aria-label=\"chart\" id=\"chart1\" style=\"width:30em !important; height:20em !important;\"></canvas></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Chart 1</b>: Pentecostal periods and their respective days. Chart built using <a href=\"#\" onclick=\"htCleanSources(); htFillReferenceSource('b77ec8fc-c988-45f3-82f8-03b136bf1aa9'); return false;\">Chart JS</a>.</div></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "#### Reasons for Modification\n\nGrouping days into such large blocks makes it hard to measure the solar year precisely. Besides needing 15 extra days to complete the cycle, the Pentecostal Calendar has another problem: it does not line up with the seasons as we know them, that is, the meteorological seasons, which typically last (<htcite0>):\n- about 90 days in winter (91 in leap years, which have 366 days);\n- up to 92 days in spring and summer.\n\nTo gain accuracy, then, we need to work with smaller groups of days.",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "9027ea5d-07f1-459c-9432-4976d7fe698d",
                     "text": "National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "2",
                        "day": "27"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_week",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Using Weeks\n\nThe proposal by Lewy Hildegard and Lewy Julius about the origin of the week (<htcite0>) is not universally accepted (<htcite1>), which, as Dr. Joshua J. Mark notes, was to be expected (<htcite2>). We will not go into the details of the disagreement, but it is worth emphasizing that knowledge advances through the exchange of different ideas and measurements.\n\nWith that in mind, we invite you—reader or listener—to pause and reflect before moving on:",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "d3baf59f-4cff-4f57-ab9f-5d35143b993a",
                     "text": "LEWY, H., & LEWY, J.",
                     "page": "pp. 50 - 51, 84",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "1942",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "21f0324e-070e-4a4b-9bd3-a04b71fa90bf",
                     "text": "Ben-Dov, J.",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2012",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "170e2f48-57f2-4a74-ae07-547e10a367b0",
                     "text": "Mark, J. J.",
                     "page": "Amorite",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2011",
                        "month": "4",
                        "day": "28"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": [
                  {
                     "type": "gregory",
                     "year": "2010",
                     "month": "",
                     "day": ""
                  },
                  {
                     "type": "unix",
                     "year": "",
                     "month": "",
                     "day": ""
                  },
                  {
                     "type": "julian",
                     "year": "",
                     "month": "",
                     "day": ""
                  }
               ],
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": "."
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection 2: Reversing</b><br /><br />Based on <b>Calculation 1</b>, how would you use smaller numbers to group the days of the year?</div></div></center>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "#### Annual Period\n\nWe know that seven periods of 50 days come close to the total number of days in a year (7 × 50 = 350). As we studied earlier, changing the order of the factors does not change the product (<htcite0>). So we can work with 50 periods of 7 days—that is, weeks—and keep the same approximation (50 × 7 = 350).\n\nThis simple reversal, however, is still not enough to cover every day of the year. To make up something like the 15 days of the *šapattum* needed to complete the annual cycle (<htcite1>), we can use the following multiplication, which also appears in the tables:",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "fbd5bb5a-58fd-4c14-a7b8-7cab2ed2560b",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Inversion",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "d3baf59f-4cff-4f57-ab9f-5d35143b993a",
                     "text": "LEWY, H., & LEWY, J.",
                     "page": "pp. 50 - 51, 84",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "1942",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p style=\"text-align=center\"><center>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; <span class=\"multexample\" id=\"mmtc3\">2</span> <br />× &nbsp;&nbsp;<span class=\"multexample\" id=\"mmbc3\">7 </span><br />_____<br /></span>&nbsp; <span class=\"multexample\" id=\"mmrc3\">14</span></center></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Calculation 3</b>: Multiplication of 2 by 7.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "As we saw in the previous text (<htcite0>), we can add the partial results:",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "4ee22ab8-e17c-48a8-8289-c8538aba77bf",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Rereading",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><p style=\"text-align=center\"><p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>52</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">×</mo><mn>7</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mn>50</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">×</mo><mn>7</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">×</mo><mn>7</mn></math></p><p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>364</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mn>350</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mn>14</mn></math></p><p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>364</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mn>364</mn></math></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Calculation 4</b>: Calculating the number of days in 52 weeks.</p></div></div></center>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "From these results we see that organizing the year into seven-day weeks comes close to the 365 days and 6 hours of a solar year (<htcite0>), but it still leaves a difference of 1 day and 6 hours. According to Lewy Hildegard and Lewy Julius, people in antiquity had already noticed this difference and dealt with it by adding an 8-day *šapattum* (<htcite1>).\n\n#### The Seasons\n\nShortening the period to seven-day weeks also helps us approximate the meteorological seasons, which last about 90 days on average (<htcite2>). We can represent that number with 13 weeks, as the following calculation shows:",
